{"name":"Spicy Hummus","hex":"#eebbaa","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"spicy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of, pertaining to, or containing spice.","Of a flavor, provoking a burning sensation due to the presence of capsaicin or a similar chemical."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/spicy"},{"term":"hummus","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A Levantine Arab dip made of chickpea paste with various additions, such as olive oil, fresh garlic, lemon juice, and tahini, often eaten with pitta bread, or as a meze."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hummus"}],"entries":[{"title":"Pungency","blocks":[{"text":"Pungency, commonly referred to as spiciness, hotness or heat, is a sensation that contributes to the flavor of certain foods such as chili peppers. Highly pungent foods may be experienced as unpleasant. The term piquancy is sometimes applied to foods with a lower degree of pungency that are \"agreeably stimulating to the palate\". In addition to chili peppers, piquant ingredients include wasabi, horseradish and mustard. The primary substances responsible for pungency are capsaicin, piperine and allyl isothiocyanate."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/PepperswithscovilleCentralMarketHoustonTX.JPG/330px-PepperswithscovilleCentralMarketHoustonTX.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pungency"},{"title":"Hummus","blocks":[{"text":"Hummus is a Levantine dip, spread, or savory dish made from cooked, mashed chickpeas blended with tahini, lemon juice, and garlic. The standard garnish includes olive oil, a few whole chickpeas, parsley, and paprika."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/Lebanese_style_hummus.jpg/330px-Lebanese_style_hummus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hummus"}]